# === Vendored third-party sources (regenerate via scripts) ===
#
# Each module owns its own vendor dir. The bootstrap scripts are
# task-scoped scripts/ and built into the modules that need them.
#
# Why vendor/ is ignored: the sqlite3 amalgamation (~10 MB of binary-like
# C data), the MinGW symlink farm at vendor/sqlite3/libc-windows-amd64/
# (~2 552 symlinks), the prebuilt libsqlite3.a / libcurl.a archives for
# each target, and the Windows libcurl-x64.dll are all very large and
# bloat every `git clone` / `git checkout`. They can be regenerated
# from a fresh checkout by running the bootstrap script:
#
#   bash scripts/bootstrap-vendor.sh        # runs all per-module fetch scripts
#
# The build.zig auto-fetches the curl vendor via `fetch-vendor-curl` step
# (no-op if already built).
/vendor/
/src/modules/custom_http_client/vendor/
# (sqlite3 amalgamation moved out with ruangsql — now an external URL
# dependency. Its own repo owns vendor/sqlite3/ + the fetch script.)
# webview/webview vendored source IS committed (small: 1 header + 1 TU +
# LICENSE) — exempt it from the blanket /vendor/ ignore above.
!/vendor/webview/
# lua/Lua 5.4 vendored source IS committed (~60 C/H files, ~1.3 MB, MIT —
# see vendor/lua/README.vendor). Hooks embed it on every target, so it
# must be present from a fresh clone with no fetch step.
# NOTE: like webview above, the !-exemption below cannot pierce the
# blanket /vendor/ dir-ignore for untracked files (verified) — the lua
# files were added with `git add -f` and stay visible because they are
# tracked. Use -f again if you ever add files under vendor/lua/.
!/vendor/lua/

# === Local task database (per-developer, not shared) ===
.pabrik/tasks/

